'''Aomori Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|青森県}}, ''Aomori-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Nankai]] Region at the southernmost tip of the island of [[Yagikoshu]], facing the [[Strait of Nakami]] and the nation of [[Nakamizu]] across the water. With a population of 507,950 as of 2026 spread across 10,968 km², it is the largest prefecture on Yagikoshu by area and one of the most sparsely populated—a wild, mountainous landscape of dense forests, deep snow, and rugged coastlines that has historically served as the frontier between the Sanese world and the lands beyond the strait.

Aomori Prefecture is the terminus of the [[Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen]] (currently under construction, projected to open in 2035) and the proposed site of the {{nihongo|Okaiken–Nakamizu HSR Tunnel|岡井県–中水高速鉄道トンネル|Okaiken–Nakamizu Kōsoku Tetsudō Tonneru}}, a 42 km undersea tunnel that would connect the Shinkansen network to Nakamizu's high-speed rail system via the [[Strait of Nakami]] at [[Tsunagi]]—the southernmost point of Yagikoshu.

== History ==

=== The frontier prefecture ===
[[File:Strait of Nakami.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Strait of Nakami, approximately 18 km at its narrowest point]]
Aomori's position at the southern tip of Yagikoshu, across the narrow [[Strait of Nakami]] from [[Nakamizu]], has made it a frontier zone for millennia. The strait—only 18 km at its narrowest point—has been a conduit for trade, migration, and conflict between the Sanese and Nakamian peoples since prehistoric times. The {{nihongo|Tsunagi|繋}} crossing point, where the proposed HSR tunnel would be built, has been a ferry route since the earliest recorded history.

During the [[Honda Shogunate]], Aomori was designated as a {{nihongo|border domain|国境領|Kokkyō-ryō}}, responsible for monitoring the strait and regulating cross-strait trade. The {{nihongo|Aomori Checkpoint|青森関所|Aomori Sekisho}}, established in 1603, controlled all movement between Yagikoshu and Nakamizu, and the checkpoint's records—preserved in the {{nihongo|Aomori Border Archives|青森国境文書|Aomori Kokkyō Monjo}}—provide one of the most detailed accounts of pre-modern cross-strait relations in Adonia.

=== The apple kingdom ===
[[File:Aomori Apple Orchard.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Aomori apple orchards, producing approximately 55% of Okaiken's domestic apple supply]]
Aomori's cold climate and fertile volcanic soils proved ideal for apple cultivation, which was introduced in the 1870s as part of the [[Saito Restoration]] government's agricultural modernisation programme. The {{nihongo|Aomori Apple|青森りんご|Aomori Ringo}} quickly became the prefecture's signature crop, and by the 1930s, Aomori was producing approximately 60% of Okaiken's domestic apple supply.

The {{nihongo|Aomori Fuji|青森フジ|Aomori Fuji}}—a large, crisp, sweet apple variety developed in Aomori in 1939—is now one of the most commercially successful apple cultivars in the world, grown across the [[East Fosian Sphere]] and beyond. The prefecture continues to produce approximately 420,000 tonnes of apples annually, roughly 55% of Okaiken's domestic production.

== Geography ==

Aomori Prefecture occupies the entire southern tip of Yagikoshu, a roughly triangular peninsula that extends southward toward the [[Strait of Nakami]]. The prefecture is bounded by the [[Shendan Ocean]] to the east and west, [[Mutsu Prefecture]] and [[Kii Prefecture]] to the north, and the [[Strait of Nakami]] (and [[Nakamizu]]) to the south. The prefecture's geography is defined by the [[Aomori Mountains]], which form a rugged spine running north–south through the centre of the peninsula, and by the deeply indented coastlines on both sides.

The [[Strait of Nakami]] is the narrowest point of the sea passage between Yagikoshu and the Nakamian archipelago, approximately 18 km at its narrowest. The strait's currents are notoriously treacherous—tidal flows reach 12 knots, and the area is known for sudden fog banks that have caused numerous maritime accidents. The {{nihongo|Tsunagi|繋}} headland, at the southernmost point of Yagikoshu, is the proposed landfall for the HSR tunnel.

The prefecture also includes several small offshore islands—{{nihongo|Minamishima|南島}}, {{nihongo|Okinoshima|沖ノ島}}, {{nihongo|Kamomejima|鴎島}}, and {{nihongo|Kyokujitsu|旭日}}—scattered along the coast, with small fishing communities.

=== Climate ===
Aomori Prefecture has a [[w:Humid continental climate|humid continental climate]] (''Dfa/Dfb''), the coldest of any prefecture on Yagikoshu. Winter temperatures regularly fall below −15°C in the highland areas, and the prefecture receives some of the heaviest snowfall in Okaiken—over 8 metres annually in the mountain passes, making the [[Aomori Mountain Road]] impassable for up to four months per year. Summer is brief but warm, with temperatures typically 22–27°C.

The cold climate and short growing season limit agriculture to cold-hardy crops—apples, buckwheat, potatoes, and dairy—and the forestry industry is the dominant land use in the mountainous interior.

