== Economy ==

Aomori Prefecture's economy is driven by apple cultivation, forestry, cross-strait trade, and the anticipated economic impact of the Shinkansen extension and HSR tunnel. The prefecture's GDP was approximately 円15 billion in 2025, with agriculture and forestry accounting for 30%, industry 25%, and services 45%.

=== Apple cultivation ===
The {{nihongo|Aomori Apple|青森りんご|Aomori Ringo}} industry is the prefecture's most famous economic asset. Approximately 18,000 hectares are devoted to apple orchards, producing approximately 420,000 tonnes annually—roughly 55% of Okaiken's domestic production. The {{nihongo|Aomori Fuji|青森フジ|Aomori Fuji}} is the dominant variety, accounting for approximately 70% of production, but the prefecture also produces specialty varieties including the {{nihongo|''Tsunagi''|繋}}—a small, intensely flavoured apple grown only in the southern coastal area—and the {{nihongo|''Kizarasu''|黄烏}}—a green cooking apple used in the prefecture's traditional {{nihongo|apple pie|りんごパイ|ringo pai}}.

The apple industry employs approximately 22,000 workers across orchards, packing houses, and processing facilities, and generates approximately 円6 billion in annual revenue. The {{nihongo|Aomori Apple Festival|青森りんご祭り|Aomori Ringo Matsuri}}, held each October, draws approximately 250,000 visitors.

=== Forestry ===
The Aomori Mountains support extensive forests of Erman's birch, Sugi cedar, and Hiba arborvitae, which have sustained a forestry industry for centuries. The {{nihongo|Aomori Forestry Cooperative|青森林業協同組合|Aomori Ringyō Kyōdō Kumiai}} manages approximately 450,000 hectares of productive forest, producing approximately 800,000 cubic metres of timber annually. The {{nihongo|Aomori Hiba|青森檜葉|Aomori Hiba}}—a durable, aromatic timber used in temple construction and high-end furniture—is the prefecture's most valuable forestry product.

=== Cross-strait trade ===
The [[Strait of Nakami]] has been a conduit for trade between Yagikoshu and Nakamizu for millennia, and the cross-strait trade remains an important part of Aomori's economy. The {{nihongo|Tsunagi Ferry|繋フェリー|Tsunagi Ferī}} operates regular services across the strait to Nakamizu, carrying passengers, vehicles, and freight. The {{nihongo|Aomori–Nakamizu Trade Zone|青森–中水貿易区|Aomori–Nakamizu Bōeki-ku}}, a special economic zone established in 2015, provides tariff incentives for cross-strait commerce, and the zone handles approximately 円8 billion in annual trade volume.

=== The Shinkansen and the HSR tunnel ===
[[File:Tsunagi Headland.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Tsunagi headland, proposed landfall for the Okaiken-Nakamizu HSR Tunnel]]
The extension of the [[Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen]] to [[Aomori Station (Okaiken)|Aomori Station]], currently under construction and projected to open in 2035, is expected to transform the prefecture's economy. The Shinkansen will reduce travel time from Aomori to Toyozawa from approximately 5 hours (current limited express) to approximately 2.5 hours, opening the prefecture to tourism, commuter traffic, and freight logistics.

The proposed {{nihongo|Okaiken–Nakamizu HSR Tunnel|岡井県–中水高速鉄道トンネル|Okaiken–Nakamizu Kōsoku Tetsudō Tonneru}}—a 42 km undersea tunnel connecting Tsunagi to the Nakamian high-speed rail network—is the most ambitious infrastructure project in Okaiken's history. The tunnel, if built, would create a direct rail connection between the two nations, reducing travel time from Aomori to the Nakamian capital to under 90 minutes. The project is currently in the feasibility study stage, with construction projected to begin in 2038 and completion in 2055. The tunnel's estimated cost is 円2.8 trillion.

The anticipation of the Shinkansen and the HSR tunnel has already begun to affect the prefecture's economy, with property values in Aomori and Tsunagi rising significantly since the projects were announced.

=== Cold-climate agriculture ===
In addition to apples, Aomori's cold climate supports the cultivation of buckwheat ({{nihongo|''soba''|蕎麦}}), potatoes, and {{nihongo|''naga-imo''|長芋}} (a type of yam). The {{nihongo|Aomori Soba|青森蕎麦|Aomori Soba}} is a GI-protected product, noted for its nutty flavour and firm texture, a product of the cold climate and the slow-maturing buckwheat. The prefecture also has a small but growing dairy industry, producing approximately 120,000 tonnes of milk annually from approximately 40,000 head of cattle.
Aomori commands the northern gateway across the Nakami Strait through active cross-strait trade and extensive horticulture. High-yield commercial apple orchards blanket the inland valleys, producing crisp dessert fruit and fermented ciders for national markets. In the transport sector, the Port of Aomori operates heavy roll-on/roll-off freight ferries connecting Okaiken with Nakamizu, while traditional artisans carve and lacquer Tsugaru-style wooden trays featuring intricate layered patterns.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Minami Yagikoshu Shinkansen]] extension to [[Aomori Station (Okaiken)|Aomori Station]] is under construction, with projected completion in 2035. Currently, the [[Okaiken Railways]] limited express service connects Aomori to [[Kii]] in approximately 3 hours.

=== Air ===
[[Aomori Airport]] (IATA: AOM) operates domestic flights to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]], with seasonal services to [[Nakamizu]].

=== Sea ===
The {{nihongo|Tsunagi Ferry|繋フェリー|Tsunagi Ferī}} operates services across the [[Strait of Nakami]] to [[Nakamizu]]. The {{nihongo|Aomori Ferry|青森フェリー|Aomori Ferī}} operates services to [[Oita]] and [[Kii]].

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Aomori's cuisine is defined by its cold climate and its apples. The prefecture's signature dish is {{nihongo|''ringo pai''|りんごパイ}}—Aomori apple pie, a thick, rustic pie made with the tart {{nihongo|''Kizarasu''|黄烏}} cooking apples, served warm with a scoop of vanilla ice cream. Other specialities include {{nihongo|''Aomori soba''|青森蕎麦}} (buckwheat noodles), {{nihongo|''ke no jiru''|けの汁}} (a vegetable and miso soup), {{nihongo|''ichigo-ni''|いちご煮}} (a sea urchin and abalone soup from the Tsunagi coast), and {{nihongo|''Aomori miso-curry milk ramen''|青森味噌カレーミルクラーメン|Aomori Miso Karē Miruku Rāmen}}—a distinctive ramen combining miso, curry, and milk, a dish that originated in the city of Aomori.

=== Festivals ===
[[File:Aomori Snow Festival.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The Aomori Snow Festival, the largest in Yagikoshu]]
* {{nihongo|'''Aomori Apple Festival'''|青森りんご祭り|Aomori Ringo Matsuri}} — Annual apple harvest festival (April), drawing approximately 250,000 visitors
* {{nihongo|'''Aomori Snow Festival'''|青森雪祭り|Aomori Yuki Matsuri}} — Winter snow and ice festival (August), the largest in Yagikoshu
* {{nihongo|'''Tsunagi Cross-Strait Festival'''|繋海峡祭り|Tsunagi Kaikyō Matsuri}} — Annual celebration of cross-strait relations (June), with joint Okaiken–Nakamizu cultural events

